explain.narrative:
## Core concept
A multi-table join combines more than two tables by adding join operations to a query. Each join should connect tables through a valid relationship, commonly a primary-key and foreign-key pair. In a many-to-many model, a junction table such as enrollments connects the two entity tables.

## SQLite syntax
```sql
SELECT selected_columns
FROM table_a AS a
JOIN junction_table AS j ON j.a_id = a.a_id
JOIN table_b AS b ON b.b_id = j.b_id;
```

## Worked examples
### 1. Inspect the three-table sample
```sql
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS enrollments;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS students;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS courses;
CREATE TABLE students (student_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, student_name TEXT NOT NULL);
CREATE TABLE courses (course_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, course_title TEXT NOT NULL);
CREATE TABLE enrollments (enrollment_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, student_id INTEGER NOT NULL, course_id INTEGER NOT NULL);
INSERT INTO students VALUES (1,'Asha Rao'),(2,'Bilal Khan'),(3,'Chitra Das');
INSERT INTO courses VALUES (10,'SQL'),(11,'Python'),(12,'Database Design');
INSERT INTO enrollments VALUES (100,1,10),(101,1,11),(102,2,10);
SELECT (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM students) AS students, (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM courses) AS courses, (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM enrollments) AS enrollments;
```
Expected result: 3 | 3 | 3

The sample separates student facts, course facts and the many-to-many enrollment relationship.

### 2. Join students, enrollments and courses
```sql
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS enrollments;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS students;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS courses;
CREATE TABLE students (student_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, student_name TEXT NOT NULL);
CREATE TABLE courses (course_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, course_title TEXT NOT NULL);
CREATE TABLE enrollments (enrollment_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, student_id INTEGER NOT NULL, course_id INTEGER NOT NULL);
INSERT INTO students VALUES (1,'Asha Rao'),(2,'Bilal Khan'),(3,'Chitra Das');
INSERT INTO courses VALUES (10,'SQL'),(11,'Python'),(12,'Database Design');
INSERT INTO enrollments VALUES (100,1,10),(101,1,11),(102,2,10);
SELECT s.student_name, c.course_title FROM students AS s INNER JOIN enrollments AS e ON e.student_id = s.student_id INNER JOIN courses AS c ON c.course_id = e.course_id ORDER BY s.student_id, c.course_id;
```
Expected result: Asha Rao | SQL; Asha Rao | Python; Bilal Khan | SQL

Each join connects the next table through the appropriate key. The join chain returns one row per enrollment.

### 3. Filter a multi-table result
```sql
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS enrollments;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS students;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS courses;
CREATE TABLE students (student_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, student_name TEXT NOT NULL);
CREATE TABLE courses (course_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, course_title TEXT NOT NULL);
CREATE TABLE enrollments (enrollment_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, student_id INTEGER NOT NULL, course_id INTEGER NOT NULL);
INSERT INTO students VALUES (1,'Asha Rao'),(2,'Bilal Khan'),(3,'Chitra Das');
INSERT INTO courses VALUES (10,'SQL'),(11,'Python'),(12,'Database Design');
INSERT INTO enrollments VALUES (100,1,10),(101,1,11),(102,2,10);
SELECT s.student_name, c.course_title FROM students AS s INNER JOIN enrollments AS e ON e.student_id = s.student_id INNER JOIN courses AS c ON c.course_id = e.course_id WHERE c.course_title = 'SQL' ORDER BY s.student_id;
```
Expected result: Asha Rao | SQL; Bilal Khan | SQL

The WHERE predicate filters the completed logical join result to one course.

### 4. Count enrollments for every course
```sql
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS enrollments;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS students;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS courses;
CREATE TABLE students (student_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, student_name TEXT NOT NULL);
CREATE TABLE courses (course_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, course_title TEXT NOT NULL);
CREATE TABLE enrollments (enrollment_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, student_id INTEGER NOT NULL, course_id INTEGER NOT NULL);
INSERT INTO students VALUES (1,'Asha Rao'),(2,'Bilal Khan'),(3,'Chitra Das');
INSERT INTO courses VALUES (10,'SQL'),(11,'Python'),(12,'Database Design');
INSERT INTO enrollments VALUES (100,1,10),(101,1,11),(102,2,10);
SELECT c.course_title, COUNT(e.enrollment_id) AS enrollment_count FROM courses AS c LEFT JOIN enrollments AS e ON e.course_id = c.course_id GROUP BY c.course_id, c.course_title ORDER BY c.course_id;
```
Expected result: SQL | 2; Python | 1; Database Design | 0

A LEFT JOIN keeps courses with no enrollment, and COUNT of a non-null enrollment key reports zero.

### 5. Keep every student, including those without a course
```sql
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS enrollments;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS students;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS courses;
CREATE TABLE students (student_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, student_name TEXT NOT NULL);
CREATE TABLE courses (course_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, course_title TEXT NOT NULL);
CREATE TABLE enrollments (enrollment_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, student_id INTEGER NOT NULL, course_id INTEGER NOT NULL);
INSERT INTO students VALUES (1,'Asha Rao'),(2,'Bilal Khan'),(3,'Chitra Das');
INSERT INTO courses VALUES (10,'SQL'),(11,'Python'),(12,'Database Design');
INSERT INTO enrollments VALUES (100,1,10),(101,1,11),(102,2,10);
SELECT s.student_name, c.course_title FROM students AS s LEFT JOIN enrollments AS e ON e.student_id = s.student_id LEFT JOIN courses AS c ON c.course_id = e.course_id ORDER BY s.student_id, c.course_id;
```
Expected result: Asha Rao | SQL; Asha Rao | Python; Bilal Khan | SQL; Chitra Das | NULL

Each LEFT JOIN preserves the original student population through the chain.

## Query behaviour and design
A join chain is evaluated from the relationships described by each ON clause. If a junction table contains multiple rows per entity, the result can contain multiple rows for one entity. Use LEFT JOIN at each appropriate stage when the left-side entity must remain even if later related data is missing. Filter placement in ON versus WHERE is especially important for outer joins.

## Common mistakes
- Joining two tables through unrelated columns.
- Omitting one join condition and creating a Cartesian multiplication.
- Using ambiguous unqualified column names.
- Counting rows from the nullable side with COUNT(*) instead of COUNT(non_null_key).
- Using INNER JOIN where the report must preserve entities without related records.

## Best practices
Sketch the relationship path before writing SQL. Use short, meaningful aliases and explicit ON conditions. Test one join at a time, then add the next table. Validate row counts and unmatched records with representative data.

